// 5.191 (G7): a progressive-cavity pump as a row of the 1D network.
//
// The machine's own law is q = n V - C dp: what one revolution sweeps minus
// the slip that leaks back across the cavities. Written as a head it is
//   H(q) = (nV - q) / (C rho g),
// linear and decreasing in q, which is the same monotone family the
// centrifugal curve sits in -- so the row's rate bracket needs no new branch.
// In this closed loop both node pressures are equal, so the row's
// differential is zero and the rate solves the machine against the wall:
//   R q = (nV - q) / C,  R = 128 mu L / (pi D^4),
// i.e. q* = nV / (1 + R C) and a rise of exactly R q*.
//   rho 850, mu 0.1, L 1000, D 0.05, nV = 1.2e-5 x 25/3 = 1.0e-4,
//   C = 1e-12 -> R C = 6.51979e-4
// Measured: q* = 9.993485e-5 m3/s, Re = 21.6 (laminar),
//           dp = 6.515182e4 Pa, head = 7.8146 m.
// P2 is the same pipe with no machine: its dpPcp token must not exist at all.
//
// The slug of the increment is that this is a NETWORK row, not a single
// machine card: the rate it settles on is the one Newton's nodal balance
// asked for, and the slip law is priced inside the same bracket.
fluid
{
    rho 850; mu 0.1;
}

nodes
{
    Suction  { pressure 1.0e5; }
    Discharge { pressure 1.0e5; }
    NodeA    { pressure 1.0e5; }
    NodeB    { pressure 1.0e5; }
}

pipes
{
    P1 { from Suction; to Discharge; length 1000; diameter 0.05; roughness 0;
         pcp { displacement 1.2e-5; speed 8.333333333333333; slip 1.0e-12; } }
    P2 { from NodeA; to NodeB; length 1000; diameter 0.05; roughness 0; }
}

solve { maxIter 50; tol 1e-10; }
